  "notes": "Bright, brassy uptempo country-pop with a belted chorus — \"the most consistently *up* female catalogue of the late 90s.\" Drums are the stated pattern: \"bright and compressed; 8th hats with tambourine doubling through choruses, kick on 1 and 'and-of-3'; a snare-and-crash accent on the last beat of the pre-chorus as the lift device,\" so the kick takes a 3& secondary anchor, the tambourine runs dense, and fills are pinned to the section boundary. The arrangement's energy lever is quoted and is a bass parameter, not a drum one: \"live bass in driving 8ths under choruses, quarters under verses; the 8th-note switch at the chorus is the arrangement's main energy lever\" — hence a high anticipation figure against a high root-following probability. Harmony is the stated \"I–V–vi–IV and I–IV–V, with the chorus often starting on IV,\" which is why the IV-first rotation carries real weight. Melody is \"mid-register conversational verse, chorus a fifth or sixth higher, sustained and belted; hooks are short repeated rhythmic cells.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium and the research says why: \"tempo spine documented, producer method documented, harmony generalised [LOW-CONF].\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the perc density, the crash placement and the countermelody ratio — \"tambourine doubling through choruses\" and \"steel and fiddle used as short answer licks\" are directions, and 3–6 and 0.56 are this author's reading of them.",
